    "section": "General English",
    "topic": "Reading Comprehension",
    "passage": "On April 1, 2016, the Reserve Bank of India enacted a decisive structural overhaul of the domestic credit delivery architecture by instituting the Marginal Cost of Funds based Lending Rate (MCLR) framework. For years, the central bank had grappled with asymmetric monetary policy transmission. Whenever the RBI increased the policy repo rate to quell surging inflation, commercial lenders swiftly elevated their lending rates to preserve margins. Conversely, when the monetary authority embarked on an accommodative cycle by slashing policy rates, commercial banks were notoriously sluggish in passing on rate cuts to retail and corporate borrowers, citing rigid deposit costs and legacy non-performing asset liabilities.\n\nUnder the superseded Base Rate regime operational since July 2010, commercial banks possessed substantial discretion in computing their average cost of funds. Because existing deposits locked in at historically higher interest rates were included in the calculation, a bank's overall cost of funds declined very slowly even after aggressive repo rate reductions. The MCLR methodology fundamentally severed this link by mandating that lending rates be determined primarily by the marginal cost of raising fresh deposits and borrowings rather than historical averages. Additionally, the MCLR formula incorporated a mandatory tenor premium, operational expenditure costs, and the negative carry on statutory reserve requirements (CRR and SLR).\n\nBy establishing clear reset periods\u2014typically linked to one-month, three-month, or one-year intervals\u2014the MCLR regime injected transparency and predictability into floating rate loan contracts. Borrowers could anticipate contractually when rate reductions would take effect on their equated monthly installments (EMIs). Nevertheless, while MCLR marked a monumental advance over the Base Rate, it remained an internal benchmark susceptible to bank-specific cost structures, paving the intellectual runway for the eventual transition toward external benchmarks.",
    "qText": "How did the computation of cost of funds under the Base Rate regime differ from that under MCLR?",
