    "topic": "Reading Comprehension",
    "passage": "Agriculture remains the demographic backbone of the Indian economy, employing nearly half of the national workforce despite contributing less than a fifth of the Gross Domestic Product. For decades, Indian agrarian livelihoods have been held hostage to the vagaries of the monsoon, exacerbated by price volatility and inadequate risk mitigation architecture. The historical crop insurance schemes\u2014most notably the National Agricultural Insurance Scheme (NAIS) and the Modified NAIS\u2014failed to achieve meaningful penetration due to onerous premium rates, localized coverage caps, and bureaucratic delays in claims settlement that often stretched beyond twelve to eighteen months.\n\nApproved by the Union Cabinet in January 2016, the Pradhan Mantri Fasal Bima Yojana (PMFBY) was conceptualized to overcome these structural roadblocks. The scheme instituted an unprecedentedly low, uniform premium regime for farmers: a nominal 2% for Kharif crops, 1.5% for Rabi crops, and 5% for commercial and horticultural produce. Crucially, the government removed all previous artificial capping on premium subsidies, committing the central and state exchequers to bear the entire actuarial premium deficit without scaling down the sum insured.\n\nTo expedite loss assessment, PMFBY mandated the deployment of modern technological interventions, including remote sensing satellite imagery, unmanned aerial vehicles (drones), and smartphone applications for Crop Cutting Experiments (CCEs). By linking insurance enrollment to institutional Kisan Credit Cards (KCC), the framework sought to insulate agrarian credit flows from climatic devastation. Yet, institutional challenges persist, including state government delays in releasing their matching subsidy shares and resistance from private insurers in honoring non-yield localized damage claims.",
