    "phase": "mains",
    "year": "2018",
    "section": "General English",
    "topic": "Reading Comprehension",
    "passage": "The Indian banking sector encountered one of its most severe existential challenges during the 2016\u20132018 period, characterized by economists as the 'Twin Balance Sheet' crisis. On one hand, non-performing assets (NPAs) on commercial bank balance sheets soared to unprecedented levels, crossing \u20b910 lakh crore by early 2018, with public sector banks bearing the brunt of over 85% of these stressed exposures. On the other hand, highly leveraged infrastructure and heavy-industry corporate conglomerates found themselves unable to service debts accumulated during the post-2008 boom years due to regulatory delays, cost overruns, and softening global commodity demand.\n\nFor over two decades, resolution mechanisms like Corporate Debt Restructuring (CDR), the Joint Lenders' Forum (JLF), and Strategic Debt Restructuring (SDR) had proven demonstrably ineffective, degenerating into mere 'extend-and-pretend' exercises where banks kicked the can down the road by evergreen loans. The enactment of the Insolvency and Bankruptcy Code (IBC) in 2016 radically transformed this debtor-friendly landscape into a creditor-driven resolution regime. By mandating a time-bound, 180-day resolution window (extendable up to 270 days) presided over by the National Company Law Tribunal (NCLT), the IBC created a decisive threat of operational liquidation. Promoters could no longer default on corporate obligations while retaining uninhibited management control.\n\nHowever, operationalizing the IBC was not devoid of immense systemic friction. Lenders were forced to accept substantial 'haircuts'\u2014often exceeding 50% to 60% of outstanding claims\u2014severely depleting their capital reserves and compelling the Government of India to announce a massive \u20b92.11 lakh crore public sector bank recapitalization program in late 2017. Furthermore, Section 29A was introduced via amendment to prevent wilful defaulters and erstwhile errant promoters from bidding for their own distressed assets at discounted fire-sale valuations, cementing ethical integrity at the expense of bidding competition.",
    "qText": "Why did earlier debt resolution frameworks like CDR and SDR fail to resolve the stressed asset overhang?",
