    "passage": "The default by a systemically important infrastructure financing and leasing conglomerate in late 2018 sent severe shockwaves through India's shadow banking ecosystem, precipitating a protracted liquidity squeeze that dominated the financial landscape throughout 2019. For years, Non-Banking Financial Companies (NBFCs) and Housing Finance Companies (HFCs) had aggressively expanded their loan portfolios by funding long-gestation assets\u2014such as commercial real estate developments and infrastructure projects\u2014using short-term commercial papers and mutual fund borrowing. This structural asset-liability mismatch (ALM) proved catastrophic when credit markets abruptly seized. As credit rating agencies executed cascading multi-notch downgrades, mutual funds faced heavy redemption pressures from institutional investors, completely halting the rollover of short-term commercial papers for shadow banks.\n\nThe resulting liquidity freeze revealed the profound interconnectedness between the shadow banking sector and scheduled commercial banks. With capital markets virtually shut for all but the highest-rated NBFCs, shadow lenders turned en masse to commercial banks for credit lines, straining bank balance sheets already burdened by legacy non-performing corporate loans. In response to this nascent credit contagion, the Reserve Bank of India stepped in with aggressive liquidity interventions, including targeted long-term repos, relaxed securitization norms, and specialized liquidity facilities. Concurrently, the government enacted statutory amendments bringing housing finance companies under direct RBI supervisory jurisdiction and empowering the resolution of systemically important financial service providers under the Insolvency and Bankruptcy Code. These measures stabilized the immediate crisis, yet they starkly highlighted the critical imperative of enforcing stringent asset-liability discipline across shadow banking.",
