    "section": "General English",
    "topic": "Reading Comprehension",
    "passage": "A perennial conundrum confronting Indian monetary policymakers has been the sluggish, asymmetric transmission of policy repo rate reductions to real-economy retail and corporate borrowers. Historically, internal interest rate benchmarking regimes\u2014ranging from the Prime Lending Rate (PLR) and Base Rate systems to the Marginal Cost of Funds Based Lending Rate (MCLR) introduced in 2016\u2014failed to produce swift rate cuts. Under the MCLR regime, commercial banks calculated lending rates based on their internal marginal deposit costs. Because bank deposit liabilities are locked in fixed-rate, term-maturity deposits that reprice slowly over multiple quarters, banks routinely delayed passing on central bank repo rate cuts, while promptly adjusting lending rates when monetary policy tightened.\n\nTo dismantle this persistent transmission bottleneck, the Reserve Bank of India enacted a decisive structural reform in late 2019 by mandating the External Benchmark Lending Rate (EBLR) framework. Effective October 1, 2019, commercial banks were compelled to anchor all new floating-rate loans to personal, retail, and micro & small enterprise borrowers against transparent external market benchmarks\u2014such as the RBI policy repo rate or Government of India Treasury Bill yields published by FBIL. Furthermore, banks were prohibited from adjusting their lending spreads for at least three years unless the borrower's credit risk profile underwent substantial deterioration. While the EBLR regime has dramatically accelerated interest rate transmission for new borrowers, it has concurrently heightened interest rate volatility on bank margins, compelling treasury desks to cultivate sophisticated asset-liability hedging mechanisms.",
