    "topic": "Reading Comprehension",
    "passage": "The mega consolidation of ten public sector banks into four anchor institutions on April 1, 2020, represents one of the most ambitious structural reorganizations in India's modern financial history. The strategic imperative behind the consolidation was compelling: to forge financially robust, well-capitalized institutions equipped with massive balance sheets capable of underwriting mega infrastructure projects and competing effectively on the global stage. By pooling branch footprints, rationalizing redundant administrative overheads, and optimizing treasury management, the amalgamated entities were envisaged to achieve economies of scale and significantly improve operational cost-to-income ratios.\n\nNonetheless, executing bank mergers amidst an unprecedented public health crisis presented formidable administrative and technological hurdles. Foremost among these was the harmonization of disparate Core Banking Solutions (CBS). Synchronizing institutions operating on different software platforms\u2014such as migrating databases between Finacle and BaNCS\u2014demanded meticulous data mapping to avert catastrophic disruptions in automated clearing, RTGS processing, and retail customer service. Furthermore, human resource frictions posed delicate governance challenges. Aligning divergent organizational cultures, harmonizing disparate employee promotion policies, and integrating uneven seniority lists required empathetic, transparent leadership. Ultimately, the long-term success of these mega mergers hinges on whether the consolidated entities can leverage their enhanced scale to maintain rigorous credit appraisal discipline and prevent the resurgence of legacy bad loans.",
