    "section": "General English",
    "topic": "Reading Comprehension",
    "passage": "The economic trajectory in the aftermath of the unprecedented pandemic-induced contraction revealed a deeply polarized recovery profile, widely diagnosed by economists as a 'K-shaped' divergence. While large, well-capitalized corporate conglomerates leveraged record-low interest rates, rapid digital migration, and substantial equity capital buffers to expand market share and de-leverage their balance sheets, the informal sector, micro-enterprises, and low-income wage earners faced protracted distress. Contact-intensive service industries\u2014hospitality, personal transport, entertainment, and offline retail\u2014confronted chronic demand deficits and balance sheet impairment. This asymmetry posed a profound challenge for macroeconomic policy, where headline gross domestic product growth figures masked underlying structural distress in employment creation and household consumption.\n\nTo counter this bifurcated trajectory, the Reserve Bank of India and fiscal authorities deployed calibrated targeted interventions. Rather than broad-based monetary stimulus, which risked stoking generalized inflationary pressures amidst supply-side bottlenecks, policymakers implemented sector-specific liquidity windows, enhanced credit guarantees under the ECLGS framework, and initiated targeted debt restructuring protocols under Resolution Framework 2.0. However, economists caution that sustained medium-term economic dynamism cannot rely indefinitely on liquidity lifelines. Structural rejuvenation demands vigorous public capital expenditure to crowd-in private investment, aggressive upskilling of displaced informal workers, and robust institutional reforms in commercial banking to clean legacy non-performing assets through bad-bank aggregation.",
