    "section": "General English",
    "topic": "Reading Comprehension",
    "passage": "The accelerated migration of consumer commerce toward cashless channels, coupled with the speculative proliferation of private cryptocurrencies, has impelled sovereign central banks to pioneer Central Bank Digital Currencies (CBDCs). Unlike volatile algorithmic crypto tokens or commercial bank deposits, a CBDC represents a direct sovereign liability of the central bank, functioning as the digital equivalent of fiat banknotes. In design architecture, central banks have converged on a two-tier intermediated model. Under this construct, the monetary authority issues and mints the digital legal tender, while commercial banks and licensed payment intermediaries handle retail customer onboarding, Know-Your-Customer (KYC) compliance, digital wallet hosting, and everyday transaction processing.\n\nHowever, the deployment of retail CBDCs is not devoid of systemic macroeconomic complexities. Foremost among regulatory anxieties is the specter of 'digital bank runs' during periods of financial panic. Because a central bank digital token is perceived as credit-risk-free, panicking depositors could instantaneously transfer their commercial savings balances into sovereign digital wallets at the touch of a screen, thereby depriving the commercial banking sector of stable CASA deposits and severely impairing credit creation. To mitigate this disintermediation hazard, central banks are exploring non-remunerated holdings, strict tier-based holding caps, and transaction ceilings. Furthermore, ensuring offline transaction capability across low-connectivity rural topographies remains an imperative engineering benchmark to fulfill true financial democratization.",
