    "topic": "Reading Comprehension",
    "passage": "The aggressive tightening cycle orchestrated by major global central banks to subdue multi-decade inflation peaks has unleashed severe tremors across international capital markets. Rapid consecutive hikes in benchmark interest rates abruptly ended the era of ultra-cheap money, precipitating sharp mark-to-market bond portfolio write-downs and sparking regional banking tremors. Nowhere is the stress more acute than in the commercial real estate (CRE) sector. During the pandemic, structural workplace shifts toward hybrid and remote models drove vacancy rates in metropolitan office corridors to record highs. As hundreds of billions in commercial mortgage-backed securities (CMBS) mature, developers confront an onerous refinancing wall characterized by doubled borrowing costs, punitive loan-to-value ratios, and depressed asset appraisals.\n\nThis synchronized monetary contraction has simultaneously reverberated through sovereign debt dynamics. Emerging market economies confront compounded vulnerabilities: imported inflation driven by a resilient US dollar, aggressive portfolio capital outflows, and escalating external debt servicing obligations. Although resilient domestic macro fundamentals and massive foreign exchange buffers have largely insulated the Indian economy from systemic contagion, the broader geopolitical landscape presents formidable head-winds. Geoeconomic fragmentation, escalating maritime freight disruptions in critical shipping straits, and volatile commodity cycles threaten to prolong the 'higher-for-longer' interest rate paradigm, compelling monetary policymakers to balance price stability against growth.",
