    "passage": "In the wake of acute geopolitical fragmentations and catastrophic pandemic supply shocks, the global macroeconomic order is undergoing an unprecedented structural rewiring. The orthodoxy of hyper-globalization\u2014governed for three decades solely by cost optimization, lean inventories, and concentrated geographic offshore assembly\u2014has yielded to national security imperatives. Sovereign governments and multinational corporations are aggressively pursuing 'de-risking' strategies, replacing vulnerable single-source dependencies with diversified networks termed 'friend-shoring' and 'near-shoring'. Nowhere is this tectonic transition more acute than across the critical minerals and clean-tech value chains. Rare earth elements, lithium, nickel, and cobalt constitute the lifeblood of electric vehicular mobility, high-density energy storage, and semiconductor fabrications. Yet, the extraction and chemical refining of these raw strategic inputs remain disproportionately monopolized within a narrow nexus of nations.\n\nTo counter this systemic vulnerability, major advanced economies and emerging industrial powers like India are deploying massive fiscal subsidies and plurilateral alliances, exemplified by the Minerals Security Partnership (MSP) and domestic Production-Linked Incentives (PLI). However, this retreat from multilateralism presents profound economic trade-offs. Duplicating capital-intensive manufacturing facilities across multiple regional blocs inherently introduces allocative inefficiencies, escalates unit production overheads, and fuels persistent structural inflationary pressures. Developing economies lacking fiscal headroom risk being squeezed out of green technology supply chains by protectionist subsidies erected in affluent markets. The central dilemma of modern statecraft is reconciling essential supply chain resilience with the efficiency and climate cooperation required to avert global ecological catastrophe.",
