    "passage": "The demonetization of high-denomination currency banknotes in late 2016 precipitated an unprecedented operational and structural shock across the Indian financial architecture. Over eighty-six percent of circulating cash was suddenly rendered non-negotiable legal tender, triggering marathon queues outside commercial bank branches and automated teller machines (ATMs) across the nation. Bank personnel worked around the clock to recalibrate currency dispensers, accept massive household deposits, and facilitate emergency currency exchanges under stringent regulatory limits. While short-term liquidity contraction impaired cash-intensive informal trade, agricultural mandis, and small-scale manufacturing enterprises, the intervention catalyzed an enduring behavioral pivot toward digital transactions.\n\nWith physical currency scarce, millions of consumers and merchant retail shopkeepers embraced electronic payment alternatives for the first time in their lives. Proprietary mobile wallet platforms recorded astronomical transaction volumes, while the fledgling Unified Payments Interface (UPI)\u2014developed by the National Payments Corporation of India (NPCI)\u2014began its historic ascent as a friction-free national payment infrastructure. Furthermore, formal banking institutions observed an immense surge in low-cost Current Account and Savings Account (CASA) deposits, expanding institutional lending capacity and driving structural digitization across urban and semi-urban commercial corridors.",
