    "topic": "Reading Comprehension",
    "passage": "Agriculture remains the demographic backbone of the Indian economy, employing nearly half of the domestic workforce while accounting for approximately sixteen percent of the nation's Gross Domestic Product. However, the sector is beset by chronic structural frailties: extreme vulnerability to erratic southwest monsoon precipitation, severe land fragmentation, and declining soil nutrient efficacy. Over-reliance on groundwater extraction through flood irrigation has depleted aquifers across northern granaries, while disproportionate application of chemical urea has disrupted the natural nitrogen-phosphorus-potassium (NPK) equilibrium of arable topsoil.\n\nTo build climate resilience and stabilize farm incomes, the government launched multi-pronged institutional interventions. The Soil Health Card scheme was instituted to provide farmers with localized crop-wise fertilizer recommendations, curbing unscientific over-fertilization. Concurrently, the Pradhan Mantri Krishi Sinchayee Yojana (PMKSY) champions the mantra of 'per drop more crop' by subsidizing precision micro-irrigation systems such as drip and sprinkler apparatuses. On the commercial front, agricultural marketing is being modernized via the Electronic National Agriculture Market (e-NAM), which connects agricultural produce market committees (APMCs) onto a unified pan-India digital bidding platform. Nevertheless, true agrarian revitalization requires reforming the Minimum Support Price (MSP) procurement architecture to ensure crop diversification beyond traditional paddy and wheat, while institutionalizing post-harvest cold storage supply chains.",
    "qText": "According to the passage, what structural factor severely impairs groundwater levels in agrarian granaries?",
