    "passage": "The acceleration of electric mobility represents a cornerstone in India's strategy to curtail crude oil import dependency, mitigate catastrophic urban atmospheric smog, and fulfill its global decarbonization pledges under the Paris Climate Agreement. With vehicular exhaust emissions accounting for a lion's share of particulate matter (PM2.5) in tier-1 metropolises, the national government introduced the Faster Adoption and Manufacturing of (Hybrid &) Electric Vehicles (FAME-II) policy. The framework committed substantial fiscal outlays to subsidize upfront capital expenditure for electric two-wheelers, three-wheelers, and commercial public transit bus fleets. By stimulating domestic consumer demand, policymakers aimed to catalyze economies of scale and encourage indigenous assembly of advanced chemistry cell battery packs.\n\nNevertheless, achieving mass vehicular electrification confronts formidable macroeconomic and supply-chain headwinds. Foremost is the acute vulnerability of the lithium-ion supply chain, where raw lithium, nickel, and cobalt refining remains concentrated within a handful of geographic monopolies. This geographical concentration exposes domestic automakers to volatile international commodity price shocks and shipping bottleneck disruptions. Moreover, consumer hesitation persists due to range anxiety, high upfront replacement battery costs, and the sparse distribution of high-speed public direct-current (DC) charging stations along intercity highways. To surmount these impediments, India must accelerate localized battery cell manufacturing through production-linked incentives (PLI), establish interoperable open-standard battery swapping networks, and reinforce municipal power distribution grids with smart bidirectional transformers.",
