    "topic": "Reading Comprehension",
    "passage": "Renewable energy transitions in emerging economies have ceased being merely environmental talking points; they are now urgent macroeconomic imperatives. For an energy-hungry economy like India, heavy dependence on imported crude oil and thermal coal exposes the balance of payments to sharp geopolitical shocks and foreign exchange volatility. Over the past five years, unprecedented public-private capital inflows have energized India's solar and wind generation capabilities, catapulting the country to the fourth rank globally in installed renewable power capacity. However, scaling intermittent clean energy introduces complex grid stability challenges. Solar power peaks during midday, whereas national power demand reaches its daily zenith in the evening hours when residential appliances surge. Without cost-effective, utility-scale Battery Energy Storage Systems (BESS), substantial renewable power gets curtailed or wasted. Here, the role of institutional green finance is decisive. Developing sovereign green bonds, attracting multilateral climate funds, and structuring risk-sharing guarantees from development banks can dramatically lower the high upfront capital costs. Moreover, a comprehensive transition cannot afford to ignore socio-economic equity. A truly 'just transition' must accommodate workers in coal-mining belts who face displacement by re-skilling them for green ecosystems.",
